        "Received English law entered Nigeria through colonial instruments and survives as one source among several. It comprises three strands: the common law of England, the doctrines of equity, and statutes of general application that were in force in England on 1 January 1900. Osborne CJ fixed that cutoff date in Lewis v Bankole (1908). High Court laws in the states adopt these strands subject to local circumstances, so every student of {S} starts from a mixed legal heritage.",
        "Customary law is the body of rules a community accepts as binding, unwritten and evolving, covering land tenure, marriage, succession and chieftaincy. A court enforces it only when it passes the validity tests: it must be shown to exist by evidence, apply to the parties, and not be repugnant to natural justice, equity and good conscience nor incompatible with any written law. In {S}, these tests decide whether an alleged customary rule will be applied at all.",
        "Islamic law operates in Nigeria as a distinct source, applied mainly in personal status matters such as marriage, divorce and inheritance among Muslims. Sharia Courts of Appeal exist in many northern states over questions of Islamic personal law, while the Constitution recognises Islamic law alongside customary law in several provisions. For candidates in {S}, the practical point is that the same facts may fall under custom, Islamic law or statute, and the governing system must be identified before any rule is chosen.",
